**Static array vs vector.** A static array (`int a[10]`) has a size fixed at compile time (or at allocation) and lives on the stack (or static storage). A `std::vector` owns heap memory, grows and shrinks at runtime, knows its own size, and cleans up automatically (RAII). Both give contiguous storage and O(1) indexing.

**Design.** Keep three members: `T* data`, `size_t size_`, `size_t cap_`. Growth is **geometric** (double the capacity when full), so `push_back` is **amortised O(1)**: copying n elements happens rarely, and the total over n pushes is O(n).

```cpp
template <class T>
class Vector {
  T* data_ = nullptr; size_t size_ = 0, cap_ = 0;
  void grow(size_t n) {
    T* p = static_cast<T*>(::operator new(n * sizeof(T)));
    for (size_t i = 0; i < size_; ++i) {
      new (p + i) T(std::move(data_[i]));
      data_[i].~T();
    }
    ::operator delete(data_);
    data_ = p; cap_ = n;
  }
public:
  Vector() = default;
  Vector(const Vector& o) { for (size_t i = 0; i < o.size_; ++i) push_back(o.data_[i]); }
  Vector(Vector&& o) noexcept : data_(o.data_), size_(o.size_), cap_(o.cap_) {
    o.data_ = nullptr; o.size_ = o.cap_ = 0;
  }
  Vector& operator=(Vector o) noexcept { swap(o); return *this; }
  ~Vector() { clear(); ::operator delete(data_); }
  void push_back(const T& v) {
    if (size_ == cap_) grow(cap_ ? cap_ * 2 : 1);
    new (data_ + size_++) T(v);
  }
  void pop_back() { data_[--size_].~T(); }
  T& operator[](size_t i) { return data_[i]; }
  size_t size() const { return size_; }
  void clear() { while (size_) pop_back(); }
  void swap(Vector& o) noexcept { std::swap(data_, o.data_); std::swap(size_, o.size_); std::swap(cap_, o.cap_); }
};
```

**Points to mention.**

- **Rule of five**: destructor, copy and move constructors and assignments, to avoid leaks and double frees.
- Use placement `new` and explicit destructor calls so that `T` need not be default-constructible.
- Growing **invalidates** iterators, pointers and references.
- `reserve(n)` avoids reallocations; `shrink_to_fit` is a non-binding request.
- `emplace_back` constructs in place; make moves `noexcept` so `std::vector` can move rather than copy on growth.

**LRU cache.** `unordered_map<Key, list<pair<Key,Value>>::iterator>` plus a `std::list` ordered by recency: `get` and `put` move the node to the front, and eviction removes the back. Both O(1).
